A||||2||madunham||"Ultimate dog bones"
Z||000000||madunham||05-04-99||01:16 PM||||I have an '87 GT auto.  Only 52k miles, and pretty much cherry.  I'm the second owner, and bought the car with only 31,700 mi., three years ago.  Little by little, I'm trying to improve the car, not "customize" it.  I've noticed an extensive advocation of poly bushings.  My dog bone seems a bit mushy, and I wonder if $45 for an "ultimate" is something that will make a noticeable improvement in power-to-the-ground, smoother shifts, etc.  My car shifts to second like it has a shift kit - I mean BANG, it's in second! Is that due to the engine moving side to side so much (in other words, is that what I'm feeling in the shift?)?  Or is the dog bone just a good idea for reducing wear and tear on motor mounts, etc.?
Z||000001||theogre||05-04-99||02:34 PM||||for comparison,,, a stock one cost me 35-40$us a few months ago.... i'll get the poly next time... <p>The stock bone is extremely soft... if you put in the poly one make sure the rest of the motor mounts are good. keep in mind... milage is not as big a factor on the mounts as age, exposure to oil/ozone, etc, all of wich act on even a parked car.<p>if you want to keep a stock look, the poly bushings to fit the stock bone are available from a couple places.
Z||000002||PaUL||05-04-99||04:35 PM||||I was thinking about using 2 old skateboard wheels. Im always looking for ways to reuse junk.
